        { "$comment": "P69 (lane cs): `selectany` — the KIND AXIS of a name whose meaning lives in `declarations[].linkageSpecifiers` (a weak definition of kind select-any), exactly as the row above is for `weak`. A VARIABLE only. ✔MEASURED, each reference alone, on a function (`__attribute__((selectany)) int f(void) { return 41; }`, and cl's own spelling): clang 18.1.3 on Linux, Apple clang, mingw-w64 gcc 13 and cl 19.51 all REFUSE it ('selectany' can only be applied to data items with external linkage; cl C2496), while gcc 13.3.0 on Linux — which implements no `selectany` and says so of every use, 'attribute directive ignored' — compiles it and the image prints 41. One working reference decides acceptance, so the declaration is accepted, the attribute is IGNORED and said to be (the decl-kind gate's warning), and the function is an ordinary strong one: the gate records the clause's name on the semantic model and the linkage fold skips a name so recorded, which is what makes the warning true of a name whose effect another tier applies. On a typedef clang accepts in silence and gcc warns; the same warning here. ⚠ NOT REACHED BY THIS ROW, stated so nobody reads the kind axis as the whole rule: a block-scope object (the two references that implement the name refuse it there; here it is passed over in silence, as every linkage attribute on a block-scope object is) and an internal-linkage object at file scope (`static` beside it is the binding conflict the linkage fold already refuses, as clang, mingw-w64 gcc and cl do).", "names": ["selectany_"], "appliesTo": ["variable"], "effect": "none" },
